      "answer": "The line and curve meet for all values of $k$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$x^2-kx+2=3x-2k$, leading to $x^2-x(k+3)+(2+2k)=0$. M1 — 3-term quadratic, may be implied in the discriminant. $b^2-4ac=(k+3)^2-8(1+k)$ (ignore “$=0$” at this stage). DM1 — cannot just be seen in the quadratic formula. $=(k-1)^2$; accept $(k-1)(k-1)$. A1 — or calculus showing minimum zero at $k=1$, or sketch of $f(k)=k^2-2k+1$. $\\geq0$, hence will meet for all values of $k$. A1 — clear conclusion. Total 4.",
